**Commentary of al-Shinqiti (Adwa' al-Bayan) on surah 22, verse 42:**

The words of His, the Exalted: ﴿And if they deny you, then the people of Noah, and ʿĀd, and Thamūd, and the people of Ibrahim, and the people of Lut had denied before them﴾ ﴿And the companions of Madyan, and Musa was denied; so I gave the disbelievers respite, then I seized them. So how was My disapproval?﴾ In these noble verses there is consolation for the Prophet (ﷺ), that what his people did to him of denial was done to other noble messengers as well. This consoles him and lightens his burden, as the Exalted said (p-267) ﴿And We relate to you the news of the messengers, by which We strengthen your heart﴾ [Hud: 120], and the verse, and His words: ﴿Nothing is said to you except what was said to the messengers before you﴾ [Fussilat: 43], and His words: ﴿And if they deny you, then messengers were denied before you﴾ [Fatir: 4], and so on through other verses.

The Exalted mentioned in these verses seven nations, each of which denied its messenger.

The first: the people of Nuh, in His words: ﴿the people of Nuh had denied before them﴾ [al-Hajj: 42]. The verses indicating the denial of the people of Nuh to their messenger are too numerous to count in the Qur'an, so we will limit ourselves to examples, given the abundance of verses indicating the denial of these nations to their messengers, such as His words: ﴿The people of Nuh denied the messengers﴾ [al-Shuʿara': 105], and His words: ﴿The people of Nuh denied before them, so they denied Our servant and said, "He is mad," and he was rejected﴾ [al-Qamar: 9], and so on through other verses.

The second: ʿĀd. The Exalted has explained, in other places in many verses, that they denied their messenger Hud, such as His words: ﴿ʿĀd denied the messengers﴾ [al-Shuʿara': 123], and His words: ﴿They said, "O Hud, you have brought us no clear proof, and we will not abandon our gods on your word, and we are not believers in you"﴾ [Hud: 53].

The third: Thamud. The Exalted has explained, in other places in many verses, their denial of their prophet Salih, such as His words: ﴿Thamud denied the messengers﴾ [al-Shuʿara': 141], and His words: ﴿So they denied him and hamstrung it﴾ [al-Shams: 14], and so on through other verses.

The fourth: the people of Ibrahim. The Exalted has explained, in other places, that they denied him in many verses, such as His words: ﴿So the answer of his people was only that they said, "Kill him or burn him," and Allah saved him from the fire﴾ [al-ʿAnkabut: 24], and His words: ﴿They said, "Burn him and help your gods"﴾ and the verse [al-Anbiya': 68], and such as His words: ﴿Do you turn away from my gods, O Ibrahim? If you do not desist, I will surely stone you, so leave me for a long time﴾ [Maryam: 46], and so on through other verses.

The fifth: the people of Lut. The Exalted has explained, in other places, that they denied him in many verses, such as His words: ﴿The people of Lut denied the messengers﴾ [al-Shuʿara': 160], and His words: ﴿So the answer of his people was only that they said, "Expel the family of Lut from your town"﴾ and the verse [al-Naml: 56], and so on through other verses.

The sixth: the companions of Madyan. The Exalted has explained that they denied their prophet Shuʿayb in other places, in many verses:

- such as His words: ﴿Away with Madyan, as Thamud went away﴾ [Hud: 95];
- and His words: ﴿And to Madyan, their brother Shuʿayb. He said, "O my people, worship Allah; you have no god other than Him"﴾ up to His words (p-268) ﴿They said, "O Shuʿayb, does your prayer command you that we abandon what our fathers worshipped, or that we do with our wealth what we wish? Indeed, you are the forbearing, the discerning one"﴾ [Hud: 84–87];
- and His words: ﴿They said, "O Shuʿayb, we do not understand much of what you say, and we see you as weak among us. Were it not for your tribe, we would have stoned you"﴾ and the verse [Hud: 91],

and so on through other verses.

The seventh: those who denied Musa, namely Fir'awn and his people. The Exalted has explained, in other places, that Fir'awn and his people denied Musa in many verses:

- such as His words: ﴿If you take a god other than Me, I will surely make you among the imprisoned﴾ [al-Shuʿara': 29];
- and His words: ﴿Did We not raise you among us as a child, and you remained among us for years of your life? And you did your deed that you did, and you were of the disbelievers﴾ [al-Shuʿara': 18–19];
- and His words: ﴿And they said, "Whatever sign you bring us to bewitch us with, we will not be believers in you"﴾ [al-Aʿraf: 132], and so on through other verses;
- and His words in this verse: ﴿So I gave the disbelievers respite, then I seized them. So how was My disapproval?﴾ [al-Hajj: 44]. The Exalted has explained the kind of punishment with which He punished each of those nations, after giving them respite and delay. He explained that He destroyed the people of Nuh by drowning in many places:

- such as His words: ﴿Then the flood seized them while they were wrongdoers﴾ [al-ʿAnkabut: 14];
- and His words: ﴿So We opened the gates of heaven with pouring water, and caused the earth to burst forth with springs, and the waters met for a matter already decreed﴾ [al-Qamar: 11–12];
- and His words: ﴿Then We drowned the rest afterward﴾ [al-Shuʿara': 120], and so on through other verses.

He explained in many places that, after giving ʿĀd respite and delay, He destroyed them with the barren wind:

- such as His words: ﴿As for ʿĀd, they were destroyed by a furious, violent wind﴾ and the verses [al-Haqqah: 6];
- and His words: ﴿And in ʿĀd, when We sent upon them the barren wind. It left nothing it came upon without making it like crumbled dust﴾ [al-Dhariyat: 41–42]; 
- and His words: ﴿Rather, it is what you sought to hasten: a wind in which is a painful punishment, destroying everything by the command of its Lord, so they became such that nothing could be seen but their dwellings﴾ [al-Ahqaf: 24–25], and so on through other verses. He explained that He destroyed Thamud with a cry that destroyed them all:

- such as His words concerning them: ﴿And those who wronged were seized by the cry, so they became prostrate in their homes﴾ [Hud: 67];
- and His words: ﴿As for Thamud, We guided them, but they preferred blindness over guidance, so the thunderbolt of humiliating punishment seized them﴾ and the verse [Fussilat: 17], and so on through other verses.

As for the people of Ibrahim who denied him, they are Nimrud and his people. The exegetes (mufassirun) have mentioned that the worldly punishment by which Allah destroyed them is what is mentioned in His words in Surat al-Nahl: ﴿Those before them had already schemed, so Allah came at their structures from the foundations, and the roof fell upon them from above, and punishment came to them from where they did not perceive﴾ [al-Nahl: 26]. The Exalted has explained that He destroyed the people of Lut by turning the highest part of their land to its lowest, and that (p-269) He sent down upon them rain of stones of sijjil in many places:

- such as His words: ﴿So when Our command came, We made its highest part its lowest, and rained upon it stones of baked clay﴾ [Hud: 82], and similar verses.

The Exalted has explained that He destroyed the companions of Madyan with the cry, in several places:

- such as His words concerning them: ﴿And the cry seized those who wronged, so they became prostrate in their homes﴾ ﴿As if they had never flourished therein. Away with Madyan, as Thamud went away﴾ [Hud: 94–95], and so on through other verses.

He explained in many places that He destroyed those who denied Musa, namely Fir'awn and his people, by drowning:

- such as His words: ﴿And leave the sea, still, for they are an army to be drowned﴾ [al-Dukhan: 24];
- and His words, the Exalted: ﴿Then Fir'awn followed them with his armies, and the sea covered them with what covered them﴾ and the verse [Ta-Ha: 78];
- and His words, the Exalted: ﴿Until, when drowning overtook him, he said, "I believe that there is no god except the One in whom the Children of Israel believe, and I am of the Muslims"﴾ [Yunus: 90],

and so on through other verses.

It is known that the verses are numerous in explaining what these seven nations, mentioned above, were destroyed by. We have mentioned a few of them as examples for the rest. All of that clarifies the meaning of His words, the Exalted, after He mentioned the denial of the seven nations to their prophets: ﴿So I gave those who disbelieved respite, then I seized them﴾ [al-Hajj: 44], i.e., with punishment. That is what we have mentioned, some of the verses indicating its details. And His words, the Exalted, in this noble verse: ﴿So how was My disapproval?﴾ "Nakir" is an ism masdar (a verbal noun form) meaning "denial/disapproval," i.e., how was My disapproval of them for their evil (munkar), which is their disbelief in Me and their denial of My messengers. That is the uprooting punishment we have explained, and after it is the punishment of the Hereafter, which does not end. We ask Allah for well-being for us and for our Muslim brothers from everything that angers our Creator and merits His punishment. The answer, your disapproval of them through that punishment, falls in its proper place in the most perfect manner, because the recompense is of the same kind as the deed. So the recompense for a deed that reached the utmost ugliness is a great retribution, a fitting recompense falling in its proper place. Glory be to the Wise, the All-Aware, who does not place a matter except in its proper place, nor brings it about except in its proper position.

Warsh alone, on the authority of Nafiʿ, read this letter-word as: ﴿fa-kayfa kana nakiri﴾ with the first-person ya' after the ra' pronounced only when connected (waslan) and not otherwise. The rest read it without the ya', being satisfied with the kasra in place of the ya'.
